{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Into their council, let not my soul come.</b> This is the deed of Zimri — when the tribe of Simeon gathered to bring the Midianite woman before Moses and said to him: Is this one forbidden or permitted? If you say forbidden — the daughter of Jethro, who permitted her to you? — let my name not be remembered in this matter. Zimri son of Salu, prince of a father's house of the Simeonites — and it does not write 'son of Jacob.'" "<b>In their assembly.</b> When Korah, who is of the tribe of Levi, assembled all the congregation against Moses and against Aaron —" "<b>Let not my honor unite.</b> There — let my name not be joined with them, as it is said ״Korah son of Izhar son of Kohath son of Levi,״ and it is not said ״son of Jacob״; but in Chronicles, when the sons of Korah were traced on the dukhnium, it is said ״son of Korah son of Izhar son of Kohath son of Levi son of Israel״ (1 Chronicles 6)." "<b>Let not my honor unite.</b> כָּבוֹד is a masculine noun, and perforce you must explain it as though speaking to the כָּבוֹד and saying: You — my honor — do not unite with them, like ״לֹא תֵחַד אִתָּם בִּקְבוּרָה״ (Isaiah 14)." "<b>For in their wrath they killed a man.</b> These are Hamor and the men of Shechem, and they are all counted as no more than one man; and so it says of Gideon: ״וְהִכִּיתָ אֶת מִדְיָן כְּאִישׁ אֶחָד״ (Judges 6), and likewise in Egypt ״סוּס וְרֹכְבוֹ רָמָה בַיָּם״ — this is its midrash. And its plain sense: many men he calls אִישׁ — each one individually; in their wrath they killed every man they were angry with, and likewise ״וַיִּלְמַד לִטְרָף טֶרֶף אָדָם אָכָל״ (Ezekiel 19)." "<b>And in their wrath they uprooted a wall.</b> They wanted to uproot Joseph, who is called שׁוֹר — as it says ״בְּכוֹר שׁוֹרוֹ הָדָר לוֹ״ (Deuteronomy 33). They uprooted — אשרי\"טיר בְּלַעַ\"ז, from the root of ״תְּעַקֵּר״ (Joshua 11)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {5 "the la'az אשרי״טיר corresponds to Old French esschartir or estrechir; Rashi cites ואת סוסיהם תעקר (Joshua 11:9, 6 in some editions), where תעקר means 'hamstring'; the lemma reads שור in the received text, Rashi glosses it as Joseph's designation, while the la'az may indicate the secondary sense 'to hamstring/cripple'."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:12.134442099Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}